  soundIntro: 'Bengali has its own beautiful alphabet. A few sounds don\'t exist in English — especially the breathy consonants and the tongue-curling retroflex sounds. Learn these and you\'ll be understood.',
  grammar: '<p>Bengali word order is flexible, but the core is <b>Subject–Object–Verb</b>: <i>ami bhat chai</i> (I rice want = "I want rice"). To say you <b>have</b> something, use <i>kache</i> (\'with\'): <i>amar kache taka achhe</i> (with me money exists). The polite <b>you</b> is <i>apni</i>; the casual <i>tumi</i>. Because verbs change with politeness, use <i>apni</i> with strangers and elders. The verb <i>achhe</i> = \'is/are there\', and <i>nei</i> = \'is not / don\'t have\'.</p>',
  bridges: '<div class="note">🇮🇳 <b>Hindi/Urdu speakers.</b> Bengali shares many Sanskrit roots with your language — <i>nam, pani, dudh</i> all feel familiar. The sounds and script differ, but a lot of core vocabulary lines up.</div><div class="note">🇬🇧 <b>English.</b> Bengali borrows many English words: <i>station, menu, table, ticket, bus</i> — you\'ll hear them in every city.</div>',
